'''''Sida spinosa''''', the '''prickly fanpetals''', is a widespread species of flowering plant in the family [[Malvaceae]].<ref name="LBJWC_SISP" >{{cite web |url=https://www.wildflower.org/plants/result.php?id_plant=SISP |title=''Sida spinosa'' |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=13 April 2023 |website=Plant Database |publisher=Lady Bird Johnson Wildflower Center |access-date=30 January 2026 }}</ref> It is native to the seasonally dry tropics and subtropics, including Latin America, the Caribbean, Africa, Madagascar, parts of the Middle East, and the Indian Subcontinent, and it has been introduced to many other locales.<ref name="POWO_235838-2" >{{cite POWO |id=235838-2 |title=''Sida spinosa'' L. |access-date=30 January 2026 }}</ref> A subshrub or herb {{cvt|0.2|to|1.0|m|in|0}} high, it is typically found in disturbed situations such as roadsides and pastures.<ref name="FNA_242348923" >{{cite web |url=http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=242348923 |title=16. ''Sida spinosa'' Linnaeus, Sp. Pl. 2: 683. 1753. False or Indian or prickly mallow, prickly sida False or Indian or prickly mallow, prickly sida |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2026 |website=Flora of North America |publisher=Missouri Botanical Garden & Harvard University Herbaria |access-date=3 January 2026 }}</ref>

